    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nConsider the following equation:\n[MATH: 2{\\color{red}m}{\\color{blue}t} = 1]\n\nContent:\nNotice that this equation has two variables, [MATH: {\\color{red}m}] and [MATH: {\\color{blue}t}.] Suppose we want to solve the equation for the variable [MATH: {\\color{blue}t}.]\n\nContent:\nTo isolate [MATH: {\\color{blue}t},] we apply the multiplication principle and divide both sides of the equation by [MATH: 2{\\color{red}m},] treating [MATH: {\\color{red}m}] as though it were a constant:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2mt|= 1; (2mt)/(2m)|= (1)/(2m); (2mt)/(2m)|= (1)/(2m); t|= (1)/(2m)]\n\nContent:\nNext, we'll take a look at an example where we need to apply both the addition and multiplication principles.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Solving Equations With Unknown Coefficients\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: 5ky-13=7] for the variable [MATH: y.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe need to isolate [MATH: y.] To do this, we first add [MATH: 13] to both sides of the equation:\n\n[MATH: 5ky \u2212 13|= 7; 5ky \u2212 13 + 13|= 7 + 13; 5ky|= 20]\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by [MATH: 5k\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: 5ky|= 20; (5ky)/(5k)|= (20)/(5k); (5ky)/(5k)|= (20)/(5k); y|= (20)/(5k)]\n\nFinally, we can further simplify the right-hand side:\n\n[MATH: y|= (5 \u22c5 4)/(5k); y|= (5 \u22c5 4)/(5k); y|= (4)/(k)]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 3kx = 5,] then [MATH: x =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (3)/(5k)]\nb. [MATH: (5)/(3k)]\nc. [MATH: 5 \u2212 3k]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (5)/(3k)]\ne. [MATH: 5 + 3k]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 2kn \u2212 3 = 5,] then [MATH: n =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (k)/(4)]\nb. [MATH: (3)/(8k)]\nc. [MATH: (4)/(k)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(8k)]\ne. [MATH: (k)/(8)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Solving Equations Containing Fractions\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: \\dfrac 34 kx + 9 = 2] for the variable [MATH: x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we multiply both sides of the equation by [MATH: 4] to get rid of the fraction:\n\n[MATH: (3)/(4)kx + 9|= 2; 4 \u22c5 ((3)/(4)kx + 9)|= 4 \u22c5 2; 3kx + 36|= 8]\n\nNow, we need to isolate [MATH: x.] To do this, we first subtract [MATH: 36] from both sides of the equation:\n\n[MATH: 3kx + 36|= 8; 3kx + 36 \u2212 36|= 8 \u2212 36; 3kx|= \u2212 28]\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by [MATH: 3k\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: 3kx|= \u2212 28; (3kx)/(3k)|= (\u2212 28)/(3k); (3kx)/(3k)|= (\u2212 28)/(3k); x|= \u2212 (28)/(3k)]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 2lx = (6)/(5),] then [MATH: x =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(3l)]\nb. [MATH: 3 + 5l]\nc. [MATH: (3)/(5l)]\nd. [MATH: 3 \u2212 5l]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (2)/(3l)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: (1)/(2)kn \u2212 5 = \u2212 2,] then [MATH: n =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: k + 6]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(6k)]\nc. [MATH: (k)/(6)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (6k)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (6)/(k)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Solving Equations Using Cross Multiplication\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the equation [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{3} = -\\dfrac{ax}{5}] for the variable [MATH: x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe need to isolate [MATH: x.] To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n[MATH: (1)/(3)|= \u2212 (ax)/(5); (1)/(3)|= (\u2212 ax)/(5); 5 \u22c5 1|= 3 \u22c5 (\u2212 ax); 5|= \u2212 3ax]\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by [MATH: -3a\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: 5|= \u2212 3ax; (5)/(\u2212 3a)|= (\u2212 3ax)/(\u2212 3a); (5)/(\u2212 3a)|= (\u2212 3ax)/(\u2212 3a); \u2212 (5)/(3a)|= x; x|= \u2212 (5)/(3a)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: (8nx)/(3) = (6)/(5),] then [MATH: x =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (9)/(20n)]\nb. [MATH: (6)/(5n)]\nc. [MATH: (8)/(3n)]\nd. [MATH: (9)/(40n)]\ne. [MATH: (9)/(10n)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: \u2212 (5zw)/(4) = (10)/(3),] then [MATH: w =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (3z)/(8)]\nb. [MATH: 8 \u2212 3z]\nc. [MATH: 8z \u2212 3]\nd. [MATH: (8z)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (8)/(3z)]"
